#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Managed headless Chromium for one sandbox member (browser contract v1).

``cloud-agent-browser start`` makes sure this member's browser runs and prints
a WebSocket URL that any CDP client (Playwright's ``connectOverCDP``,
Playwright MCP ``--cdp-endpoint``, Chrome DevTools MCP ``--wsEndpoint``,
Puppeteer ``browserWSEndpoint``) can connect to. There is deliberately no HTTP
discovery endpoint (``/json/version``): clients resolve it against the
server's root, which would drop the token from the path. ``status``,
``stop`` and ``self-test`` do what they say.

Why a daemon in front of Chromium instead of ``--remote-debugging-port``:
every member UID of a group shares the container's loopback, and Chromium's
own debug port has no authentication. So Chromium speaks CDP only over
``--remote-debugging-pipe`` to this process. This process serves it on
loopback behind a random path token, written to a 0600 file in the member's
private directory, the same loopback-plus-secret shape as the OpenCode/Kilo
bridge.

A pipe is a single CDP connection, but several tools may want the browser at
once. Each client therefore gets its own browser-level session
(``Target.attachToBrowserTarget``); its commands are sent on that session with
ids rewritten into one space, and every reply and event is routed back to the
client that owns the session it came from. A client never sees another
client's sessions. ``Browser.close`` from a client ends only that client's
connection: the browser belongs to the member, not to one tool.

The browser stops when no client has been connected for the idle timeout,
when Chromium exits, or on ``stop``. Its profile (cookies, storage) stays on
disk for the next start.

It runs isolated (``-I``), like the other helpers in this image, and uses the
standard library only.
"""

